{"name":"No More Drama","hex":"#a33f40","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"more","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Determiner","definitions":["comparative degree of many: in greater number.","comparative degree of much: in greater quantity, amount, or proportion."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["To a greater degree or extent. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Used to form the comparative form of adjectives and adverbs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/more"},{"term":"drama","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A composition, normally in prose, telling a story and intended to be represented by actors impersonating the characters and speaking the dialogue.","Such a work for television, radio or the cinema, usually one that is not a comedy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/drama"}],"entries":[{"title":"No More Drama","blocks":[{"text":"No More Drama is the fifth studio album by American singer Mary J. Blige. It was released on August 18, 2001, by MCA Records."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a7/Nomoredrama.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No_More_Drama","wikidata":"Q743469"},{"title":"No More Drama (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"No More Drama\" is a song by American recording artist Mary J. Blige. Written and produced by duo Jimmy Jam and Terry Lewis, it was initially intended for Blige's fourth studio album Mary (1999) before she insisted on making it the title track of her fifth studio album of the same name (2001). The song embodies portions of \"The Young and the Restless Theme\" (1973), written by Barry De Vorzon and Perry Botkin Jr. Lyrically, the song is about going through hard times and moving on from pain."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1d/Mary_no_more_drama_%28single%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No_More_Drama_(song)","wikidata":"Q428229"},{"title":"Drama","blocks":[{"text":"Drama is the specific mode of fiction represented in performance: a play, opera, mime, ballet, etc., performed in a theatre, or on radio or television. Considered as a genre of poetry in general, the dramatic mode has been contrasted with the epic and the lyrical modes ever since Aristotle's Poetics —the earliest work of dramatic theory."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/The_Battle_of_Bosworth_Field_-_A_Scene_from_the_Great_Drama_of_History.jpg/330px-The_Battle_of_Bosworth_Field_-_A_Scene_from_the_Great_Drama_of_History.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drama","wikidata":"Q25372"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/6c/No_More_Drama_2002.png/250px-No_More_Drama_2002.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6c/No_More_Drama_2002.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/6c/No_More_Drama_2002.png/250px-No_More_Drama_2002.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANo_More_Drama_2002.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Mary_j-02-mika.jpg/500px-Mary_j-02-mika.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Mary_j-02-mika.jpg/1280px-Mary_j-02-mika.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Mary_j-02-mika.jpg/500px-Mary_j-02-mika.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the United States, No Mo